@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Points, rubrics and feedback)

How questions are worth points, how they are marked by hand, and what a student sees after answering.

@section(Points and marking)

@subsection(@@qc)

A numbered points marker: the P points are split across the questions that follow.

@qc(10)

@qc(30, choose=3)

@qc(9, pick=3 from easy)
@questionblock(id=easy)
1+1 = @q(fn: 2)
2+2 = @q(fn: 4)
3+3 = @q(fn: 6)
4+4 = @q(fn: 8)
@end

@subsection(@@rubric)

Criteria for the question just above — one text: points per line.

@rubric
States the law: 2
Correct units: 1
@end

@rubric(name=lab)
Circuit works: 5
Write-up is clear: 3
@end

@rubric(use=lab)

@rubric(use=lab)
Explanation is convincing: 2
@end
@code(python, run, pts=20)
def total(xs):
    return 0

@subsection(@@modelans)

A reference answer for the open question above — feeds the auto-grader's semantic tiers.

@modelans
Because the current divides…
@end

@subsection(@@solution)

The teacher's WORKING PROGRAM, inside any runnable block — code, codex, a notebook cell, octo, shader, draw and the EECad studios.

@solution
def total(xs):
    return sum(xs)
@end
@tests
<<< 1 2 3
<<< 4 5
@end
@end

@subsection(@@confidence)

Certainty-based marking: the student rates their confidence, and calibration is scored.

@confidence … @end

@@cbm is the same as @@confidence: @cbm
How sure are you that 2+2=4? @q(fn: 4)
@end

@subsection(@@tolerance)

The default ± for numeric answers: a percent (5), an absolute (0.05abs), or ±0.05.

@tolerance(5)

@tolerance(0.05abs)

@@tol is the same as @@tolerance: @tol(2)

@section(Feedback)

@subsection(@@hint)

A hint ladder shown after a question — one hint per line, revealed one at a time.

@hint(penalty=1)
Start from KVL
Now sum the drops
@end

@subsection(@@branch)

An adaptive follow-up shown depending on how the previous question was answered.

@branch(if=wrong)
Review section 2.
@end
